@node Top
@top Quasar Linux Installer

This document describes the components and usage of the Quasar Linux Installer,
a collection of shell scripts that provides a modular, menu-driven installation
process for the Quasar Linux distribution.

@menu
* Introduction::               Overview and core concepts
* Directory Structure::        Layout of installer files
* Main Menu Script (main.sh):: Top-level entry point
* Core Installation Modules::  The main installation stages
* Additional Modules::         Users, region, network, chroot, recovery
* Bootloader Setup::           Installing the bootloader
* Partitioning::               Disk partitioning and formatting
* Environment Variables::      Global settings used by the scripts
* Usage Examples::             How to run the installer
* Troubleshooting::            Common issues and solutions
* Index::                      Index of topics
@end menu

@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

The Quasar Linux Installer is a set of Bash scripts that guides the user through
the installation of Quasar Linux, an Artix Linux-based distribution with a custom
OpenRC configuration, pre‑installed packages, and a user‑friendly menu interface.
It supports both UEFI and BIOS systems, and offers automated as well as manual
partitioning, multiple kernel choices, and a selection of desktop profiles.

Key features:

@itemize
@item Modular design – each step is handled by a separate script.
@item Interactive menus using @command{dialog} and @command{whiptail}.
@item Internationalisation via @code{gettext} (supports 10+ languages).
@item Auto‑detection of UEFI/BIOS, network connectivity, and disk layout.
@item Encryption support through LUKS for the root partition.
@item Three kernel flavours: Zen, LTS, and vanilla.
@item Pre‑configured OpenRC services and FastZram for swap compression.
@end itemize

@node Directory Structure
@chapter Directory Structure

The installer is installed under @file{/installer} (unless overridden by
@env{INSTALL_SCRIPT}) and relies on the SDK platform at @file{/usr/local/sdk}.
The main scripts are located as follows:

@table @file
@item /installer/run
The main entry point (symbolic link or actual script) – typically calls @file{main.sh}.
@item /installer/modules/
Contains all stage scripts: @file{parted.sh}, @file{basepack.sh}, @file{users.sh},
@file{inst_pack.sh}, @file{bootloader.sh}, @file{mirrorconfig.sh}, @file{region.sh},
and the @file{userland/} subdirectory with optional post‑install scripts.
@item /installer/profiles/
Defines different desktop/application profiles: @file{default/}, @file{gaming/},
@file{ai/}, @file{custom/} – each containing its own package lists and configuration.
@item /usr/local/sdk/
Common utilities, logo files, locale data, system tools (network, chroot, recovery).
@item /usr/local/sdk/locale/
Gettext translation files (`.mo`) for multiple languages.
@end table

@node Main Menu Script (main.sh)
@chapter Main Menu Script (@file{main.sh})

This script is the top‑level menu of the installer. It is invoked automatically
when the live system boots or when you run @file{/installer/run}. It performs
the following actions:

@menu
* Initialization::             Checking network and auto‑install
* Main Menu::                  Interactive menu items
* Menu Functions::             Detailed behaviour of each option
@end menu

@node Initialization
@section Initialization

When started, @file{main.sh}:

@itemize
@item Sets up colour variables for output (@code{RED}, @code{GREEN}, @dots{}).
@item Loads the saved language preference from @file{~/.quasar-lang}.
@item Checks network connectivity by pinging @code{quasarfoks.github.io}.
@item If network is down, runs the network configuration script.
@item Checks for the presence of @file{/installer/run} – if missing, logs an error.
@item If @file{/usr/local/sdk/auto/default.yaml} exists, it launches an automatic
      installation (@code{/installer/run --auto}) and reboots upon success.
@item Otherwise, displays the main menu.
@end itemize

@node Main Menu
@section Main Menu

The menu is displayed with a logo (if @file{mainlogo.txt} exists) and a list of options:

@example
╔════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║################    SDK function    ####################║
║  [ 1 ]   Chroot                                        ║
║  [ 2 ]   Install QuasarLinux                           ║
║  [ 3 ]   Recovery System (Timeshift)                   ║
║  [ 4 ]   Live system                                   ║
║  [ 5 ]   Language: <current>                          ║
║  [ 6 ]   Network                                       ║
║  [ 7 ]   Support                                       ║
║################    Power function    ##################║
║  [ 8 ]   Shutdown                                      ║
║  [ 9 ]   Restart                                       ║
╚════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
@end example

The menu is centered on the screen and accepts a single digit input.

@node Menu Functions
@section Menu Functions

@table @code
@item 1 Chroot
Runs @file{/usr/local/sdk/chroot/chroot.run} – a tool to enter the installed system’s chroot environment.

@item 2 Install QuasarLinux
Calls @code{start_install}, which executes @file{/installer/run} (the main installation script).
If @file{/installer/run} is missing, it logs an error and returns to the menu.

@item 3 Recovery System (Timeshift)
Runs @file{/usr/local/sdk/recovery/recov.run} – a recovery tool based on Timeshift.

@item 4 Live system
Starts an interactive shell with a custom prompt (live environment).

@item 5 Language
Loads @file{/usr/local/sdk/locale/main.sh} to change the system language.

@item 6 Network
Starts @file{/usr/local/sdk/system/network} – a network configuration utility.

@item 7 Support
Opens @file{/usr/local/sdk/HelpCenter/main.sh} – the help centre.

@item 8 Shutdown
Shuts down the system after a 3‑second delay.

@item 9 Restart
Reboots the system after a 3‑second delay.
@end table

@node Core Installation Modules
@chapter Core Installation Modules

The actual installation is driven by @file{install.sh} (located in @file{/installer/modules/}).
This script presents a menu of installation stages:

@example
Quasar-install
1 Express setup
2 Partitioning
3 Install base system
4 Install packages
5 User setup
6 Install bootloader
7 Audio
8 Wine
9 Web browser
10 Region
11 Office
12 Exit
@end example

Each option calls the corresponding module script.

@node install.sh
@section @file{install.sh} – Main Installer Menu

@itemize
@item @code{express} – runs all essential steps in sequence: partitioning, base system,
      users, packages, and bootloader.
@item @code{parted_menu} – calls @file{parted.sh} (disk partitioning).
@item @code{basepack} – calls @file{basepack.sh} (base system + kernel + services).
@item @code{inst_pack} – calls @file{inst_pack.sh} (profile selection).
@item @code{user} – calls @file{users.sh} (user creation).
@item @code{bootloader} – calls @file{bootloader.sh} (bootloader installation).
@item @code{audio_config} – runs @file{userland/audio_config.sh}.
@item @code{wine_install} – runs @file{userland/wine_config.sh}.
@item @code{browser_install} – runs @file{userland/browser_config.sh}.
@item @code{region} – calls @file{region.sh} to set the time zone.
@item @code{office_install} – runs @file{userland/office_config.sh}.
@end itemize

@node basepack.sh
@section @file{basepack.sh} – Base System Installation

This script is responsible for installing the minimal system into @file{/mnt}.

@enumerate 1
@item
Asks the user to choose a kernel (Zen, LTS, or vanilla).
@item
Installs the base packages via @command{basestrap} (terminus‑font, iptables, base, base‑devel,
mkinitcpio, openrc, dbus, elogind, linux‑firmware, dialog, acpid, flatpak, chrony, etc.).
@item
Generates @file{/mnt/usr/lib/os-release} and @file{/mnt/etc/lsb-release}.
@item
Configures OpenRC by writing @file{/mnt/etc/rc.conf} and @file{/mnt/etc/conf.d/rc}
with parallel boot optimisation.
@item
Creates @file{/mnt/etc/fstab} using @command{fstabgen -U}.
@item
Clones and installs @strong{FastZram} – a service that creates a compressed swap
device sized at 50% of RAM (capped between 1 GiB and 16 GiB).
@item
Enables the following OpenRC services:
@itemize
@item @code{udev} (sysinit)
@item @code{FastZram} (sysinit)
@item @code{dbus} (boot)
@item @code{elogind} (boot)
@item @code{acpid} (default)
@item @code{NetworkManager} (default)
@item @code{chrony} (default)
@end itemize
@item
Copies a custom @file{pacman.conf} and Pixmaps.
@item
Downloads and extracts a branding tarball (including systemd‑rc compatibility wrapper).
@item
Performs a SHA256 integrity check on the downloaded archive.
@end enumerate

The script uses @file{/installer/profiles/base/pacman.conf} for the repository configuration.

@node inst_pack.sh
@section @file{inst_pack.sh} – Package Profile Selection

This script lets the user choose a predefined package set:

@itemize
@item @code{Custom} – runs @file{profiles/custom/custom.sh}.
@item @code{AI prefix} – runs @file{profiles/ai/ai.sh}.
@item @code{Gaming prefix} – runs @file{profiles/gaming/gaming.sh}.
@item @code{Default prefix} – runs @file{profiles/default/default.sh}.
@end itemize

Each profile script installs the respective applications (e.g. AI tools, gaming
libraries, office suite) after the base system is chrooted.

@node mirrorconfig.sh
@section @file{mirrorconfig.sh} – Mirror Configuration

This script configures the @file{/mnt/etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist} based on the selected
geographical region (Europe, Asia, North America, South America, Oceania, or Global).
It writes a list of servers appropriate for that region and includes a fallback.
After generation, it suggests running @code{pacman -Syy} to refresh the database.

@node Additional Modules
@chapter Additional Modules

The installer includes several supporting scripts.

@menu
* users.sh::                   User and root password setup
* region.sh::                  Time zone selection
* Network Configuration::      Network setup (Wi‑Fi, DNS, proxy)
* Chroot Tool::                chroot.sh – enter the installed system
* Recovery Tool::              recov.sh – Timeshift recovery
@end menu

@node users.sh
@section @file{users.sh} – User and Root Password Setup

This script handles user creation inside the chroot.

@itemize
@item Mounts the necessary pseudo‑filesystems (@file{/dev}, @file{/proc}, @file{/sys}, @file{/run}) inside @file{/mnt}.
@item Asks for a username (must not be @code{root}).
@item Creates a group and user with the same name, adds the user to the @code{wheel} group.
@item Prompts for a password (twice for confirmation) and sets it via @code{chpasswd}.
@item Adds the user to @file{/etc/sudoers.d/} with full privileges.
@item Asks for the @code{root} password and sets it.
@end itemize

After completion, a success message is displayed.

@node region.sh
@section @file{region.sh} – Time Zone Selection

This script is called by the main installer menu to set the system’s time zone.
It reads the available zoneinfo directories (@file{Europe}, @file{Asia}, @file{America},
@file{Africa}, @file{Antarctica}, @file{Arctic}, @file{Atlantic}, @file{Australia},
@file{Brazil}, @file{Canada}, @file{Etc}) and presents a radiolist of sub‑regions.
The selected region is linked as @file{/mnt/etc/localtime}.

@node Network Configuration
@section Network Configuration (@file{network.sh})

The script @file{/usr/local/sdk/system/network} provides network setup functions,
including Wi‑Fi connection, DNS configuration, and proxy settings. It is used in
two contexts:

@itemize
@item During the main installer flow, it is invoked automatically when the system
      fails to reach @code{quasarfoks.github.io} (via the @code{check_network}
      function in @file{main.sh}). In this case, it is called with the argument
      @code{installation}.
@item From the main menu (option 6), where it runs interactively without the
      @code{installation} argument.
@end itemize

@menu
* Installation Mode::          Auto‑configuration during install
* Interactive Mode::           Manual configuration from menu
* Wi‑Fi Connection::           Using @command{iwctl}
* DNS Configuration::          Setting up DNS over TLS with Knot Resolver
* Proxy Configuration::        HTTP/HTTPS proxy environment variables
@end menu

@node Installation Mode
@subsection Installation Mode

When invoked with @code{installation} as the first argument, the script:

@enumerate
@item Detects if a Wi‑Fi adapter is present and prompts the user to connect.
@item If the user chooses to connect, it calls @code{connect_to_wifi}.
@item After the Wi‑Fi connection (or if skipped), it checks internet connectivity
      by pinging @code{quasarfoks.github.io}.
@item If the ping fails, a warning is displayed and the user is asked whether to
      continue anyway.
@end enumerate

If the user cancels, the installation process is aborted.

@node Interactive Mode
@subsection Interactive Mode

When run without arguments (e.g., from the main menu), the script displays a
whiptail menu with three options:

@table @code
@item 1 WiFi configuration
Calls @code{connect_to_wifi} (see below).
@item 2 DNS configuration
Calls @code{configure_dns} (see below).
@item 3 PROXY configuration
Calls @code{configure_proxy} (see below).
@end table

@node Wi‑Fi Connection
@subsection Wi‑Fi Connection

The Wi‑Fi functionality relies on @command{iwctl}, the Intel Wireless daemon.
The steps are:

@itemize
@item Scan for available networks: @code{iwctl station wlan0 scan}.
@item Display the list of networks: @code{iwctl station wlan0 get-networks}.
@item Prompt the user for the SSID and connect using
      @code{iwctl station wlan0 connect <ssid>}.
@item After successful connection, the user is asked whether to configure DNS.
@end itemize

@node DNS Configuration
@subsection DNS Configuration

The DNS configuration uses @command{knot-resolver}. The user selects a DNS
provider from a whiptail list:

@table @asis
@item Cloudflare (1.1.1.1)
@item Google (8.8.8.8)
@item Quad9 (9.9.9.9)
@item OpenDNS (208.67.222.222)
@item AdguardDNS (94.140.14.14)
@item YandexDNS (77.88.8.8)
@end table

The corresponding @code{policy.TLS_FORWARD} rules are appended to a temporary file
@file{/etc/knot-resolver/kresd.conf.dev}, which is then renamed to
@file{/etc/knot-resolver/kresd.conf}. This configures DNS over TLS for the system.

@node Proxy Configuration
@subsection Proxy Configuration

The proxy settings are interactive. The user is asked for the proxy address
in the format @code{IP:port}. Then they are asked if the proxy is open (no
authentication) or requires a login/password. Based on the answer, the appropriate
environment variables are set:

@itemize
@item @code{http_proxy}
@item @code{https_proxy}
@item @code{ftp_proxy}
@item @code{all_proxy}
@end itemize

These variables are exported in the current shell, affecting subsequent commands
run in the same session.

@noindent
The script also sets @code{no_proxy} to exclude local addresses from proxying.

@node Chroot Tool
@section Chroot Tool (@file{chroot.sh})

The script @file{/usr/local/sdk/chroot/chroot.run} (which calls @file{chroot.sh})
allows the user to enter the installed system (or any mounted Linux system) in a
chroot environment. It is invoked from the main menu (option 1).

@menu
* Chroot Mounting Partitions:: Selecting and mounting root, boot, and extra partitions
* Mounting Virtual Filesystems:: /proc, /dev, /sys, /run, and resolv.conf
* Extra Partitions::           /home, /var, /usr
* Entering the Chroot::        Running @command{chroot}
@end menu

@node Chroot Mounting Partitions
@subsection Chroot Mounting Partitions

The script first lists available disks (@code{lsblk -d}) and asks the user to
enter the disk name (e.g., @code{sda} or @code{nvme0n1}). Then it displays the
partition table and prompts for:

@itemize
@item The root partition (mandatory) – mounted at @file{/usr/local/sdk/rootdir}.
@item If UEFI mode is detected, the EFI partition is requested and mounted at
      @file{rootdir/boot/efi}.
@item In BIOS mode, a boot partition can be optionally specified (mounted at
      @file{rootdir/boot}); if left empty, it is skipped.
@end itemize

All paths are relative to the @code{rootdir} variable (@code{/usr/local/sdk/rootdir}).

@node Mounting Virtual Filesystems
@subsection Mounting Virtual Filesystems

Before entering the chroot, the script mounts the essential virtual filesystems
inside the @code{rootdir}:

@itemize
@item @command{mount --bind /proc rootdir/proc}
@item @command{mount --bind /dev rootdir/dev}
@item @command{mount --bind /run rootdir/run}
@item @command{mount --bind /sys rootdir/sys}
@end itemize

It also appends @code{nameserver 9.9.9.9} to @file{rootdir/etc/resolv.conf}
to provide DNS inside the chroot.

@node Extra Partitions
@subsection Extra Partitions

The user is asked whether they have separate partitions for @file{/home},
@file{/var}, or @file{/usr}. If yes, they are prompted for the partition name
and mounted under the respective directory inside the chroot.

@node Entering the Chroot
@subsection Entering the Chroot

Finally, @command{chroot rootdir} is executed, dropping the user into a shell
inside the mounted system.

@node Recovery Tool
@section Recovery Tool (@file{recov.sh})

The script @file{/usr/local/sdk/recovery/recov.run} (which calls @file{recov.sh})
provides a recovery interface using @command{timeshift}. It is invoked from the
main menu (option 3).

@menu
* Mounting the System::        Selecting the disk and partitions
* Listing Snapshots::          Displaying available Timeshift snapshots
* Restoring a Snapshot::       Performing the restore
@end menu

@node Mounting the System
@subsection Mounting the System

The script operates similarly to @file{chroot.sh} but mounts the target system
at @file{/mnt} (not @file{/usr/local/sdk/rootdir}). It:

@itemize
@item Asks for the disk and root partition, and mounts it at @file{/mnt}.
@item Mounts the EFI or BIOS boot partition if specified.
@item Optionally mounts separate @file{/home}, @file{/var}, or @file{/usr}
      partitions.
@item Mounts the virtual filesystems (@file{/proc}, @file{/dev}, @file{/run},
      @file{/sys}) and adds a DNS entry.
@end itemize

@node Listing Snapshots
@subsection Listing Snapshots

After mounting, the script runs @command{chroot /mnt timeshift --list} to display
all available snapshots. The user is then prompted to continue or cancel.

@node Restoring a Snapshot
@subsection Restoring a Snapshot

If the user chooses to continue, @command{chroot /mnt timeshift --restore} is
executed, which starts the interactive Timeshift restore process. After the
restore completes, the system can be rebooted.

@node Bootloader Setup
@chapter Bootloader Setup (@file{bootloader.sh})

This script detects whether the system is booted in UEFI or BIOS mode and offers
appropriate bootloader options.

@menu
* UEFI Bootloaders::           GRUB, EFISTUB, rEFInd
* BIOS Bootloaders::           GRUB, Syslinux
@end menu

@node UEFI Bootloaders
@section UEFI Bootloaders

If UEFI is detected, the user can choose:

@table @code
@item GRUB
Installs @code{grub}, @code{efibootmgr}, and @code{os-prober}. GRUB is installed
to the EFI system partition (@file{/boot/efi}) with the bootloader ID @samp{GRUB}.
The configuration is generated using @command{grub-mkconfig}.

@item EFISTUB
Uses the EFI stub loader. The script copies the kernel and initramfs into the EFI
partition and creates a boot entry with @command{efibootmgr}. It automatically
detects the installed kernel (linux, linux-zen, or linux-lts).

@item rEFInd
Installs @code{refind} and runs @command{refind-install}. rEFInd is a graphical
boot manager that also supports auto‑detection of operating systems.
@end table

@node BIOS Bootloaders
@section BIOS Bootloaders

For legacy BIOS, the options are:

@table @code
@item GRUB
Installs @code{grub} and @code{os-prober}, then runs @command{grub-install}
with the @option{--target=i386-pc} flag to write the bootloader to the Master
Boot Record.

@item Syslinux
Installs @code{syslinux}, configures the boot partition as active using
@command{parted}, and writes the MBR code. A minimal @file{syslinux.cfg}
is created that points to the detected kernel and initramfs with the correct
root UUID.
@end table

In both cases, the script mounts the necessary pseudo‑filesystems before
executing the bootloader commands.

@node Partitioning
@chapter Partitioning (@file{parted.sh})

This script handles disk partitioning, formatting, and mounting. It supports both
UEFI and BIOS systems, and can operate on a single disk or multiple disks.

@menu
* Partitioning Modes::         Auto, Manual, Replace OS
* Filesystem and Encryption::  ext4, btrfs, xfs, LUKS
* Mounting Partitions::        Root, boot, swap, home
@end menu

@node Partitioning Modes
@section Partitioning Modes

The user selects one of three modes:

@table @code
@item Auto
The script automatically creates a GPT or MBR table, then partitions the disk:
@itemize
@item UEFI: ESP (512 MiB), swap (4 GiB), root (rest).
@item BIOS: boot (512 MiB), swap (4 GiB), root (rest).
@end itemize
After partitioning, it formats the partitions, creates swap, and mounts them.

@item Manual
The user invokes @command{cfdisk} to manually partition the selected disk.
They then specify the root partition, choose a filesystem, optionally enable
LUKS encryption, and specify boot and swap partitions. Additional partitions
can be mounted as home, var, etc.

@item Replace OS (beta)
This mode attempts to back up the existing operating system. It mounts the
existing root and boot partitions, copies user home directories to
@file{/mnt/old.os/home_backup/}, and archives the entire old system (excluding
caches, swap, tmp, etc.) into a compressed tarball (tar.xz, tar.zst, or tar.gz).
The old system is then removed, leaving only the backup and the boot directory.
@end table

@node Filesystem and Encryption
@section Filesystem and Encryption

The script supports three filesystems: @code{ext4}, @code{btrfs}, and @code{xfs}.
If LUKS is chosen (in manual mode), the root partition is encrypted with
@command{cryptsetup luksFormat} and opened as @file{/dev/mapper/QuasarRoot}.
The filesystem is then created on the mapped device.

@node Mounting Partitions
@section Mounting Partitions

After formatting, the partitions are mounted:

@itemize
@item Root: @file{/mnt}
@item Boot/EFI: @file{/mnt/boot} or @file{/mnt/boot/efi}
@item Swap: activated with @command{swapon}
@item Additional partitions (e.g., home) are mounted under @file{/mnt/home} or other user‑defined mount points.
@end itemize

@node Environment Variables
@chapter Environment Variables

The following environment variables are used throughout the installer:

@table @env
@item LANG
Controls the language (e.g., @code{en_US.UTF-8}). Saved in @file{~/.quasar-lang}.
@item INSTALL_SCRIPT
Path to the installer root directory (default @file{/installer}).
@item SDK_PLATFORM
Path to the SDK utilities (default @file{/usr/local/sdk}).
@item TEXTDOMAIN
Gettext domain, usually @code{installer}.
@item TEXTDOMAINDIR
Directory containing the translation files (default @file{/usr/local/sdk/locale}).
@item UEFI_MODE
Set to @code{1} if the system is booted in UEFI mode, else @code{0}.
@item LUKS_MODE
Set to @code{1} if LUKS encryption is enabled for the root partition.
@item ROOT_UUID
UUID of the root filesystem (used in bootloader configuration).
@end table

Colour variables (@code{RED}, @code{GREEN}, @dots{}) are used for console output.

@node Usage Examples
@chapter Usage Examples

@itemize
@item
To start the installer in a live environment:
@example
/installer/run
@end example

@item
To run the auto‑install (if @file{default.yaml} exists):
@example
/installer/run --auto
@end example

@item
To manually launch the main menu without network checks:
@example
/usr/local/sdk/main.sh
@end example

@item
To install only the base system and then exit:
@example
/installer/modules/basepack.sh
@end example

@item
To set the time zone from the command line:
@example
/installer/modules/region.sh Europe
@end example

@item
To enter the chroot of a mounted system (e.g., for manual fixes):
@example
/usr/local/sdk/chroot/chroot.run
@end example

@item
To start the recovery interface:
@example
/usr/local/sdk/recovery/recov.run
@end example
@end itemize

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@table @strong
@item “RUN file is not found”
Ensure that @file{/installer/run} exists and is executable. If not, re‑copy the
installer files or run @file{/usr/local/sdk/main.sh} directly.

@item “Network is not working”
The installer tries to ping @code{quasarfoks.github.io}. If this fails, it runs
@file{/usr/local/sdk/system/network}. You can also configure the network manually.

@item “Hash check failed”
The branding archive’s SHA256 does not match the expected value. This could be
due to a corrupted download or a version mismatch. Verify the URL and try again.

@item LUKS password not accepted
Ensure you type the password correctly during @command{cryptsetup luksFormat}.
If you forget it, the data is unrecoverable.

@item Bootloader not showing after reboot
Check that the EFI partition is mounted correctly during installation and that
the bootloader entry was created. For UEFI, you may need to use the firmware
boot menu to select the boot device.

@item Services not starting
Verify that the appropriate OpenRC services are enabled: @code{rc-update show}.
Also check @file{/mnt/etc/rc.conf} for parallel boot settings.

@item Chroot fails with “cannot execute”
Make sure the virtual filesystems are mounted and the root directory contains a
working system. Also check that @command{chroot} is available.

@item Timeshift restore fails
Ensure the target system is mounted correctly and that Timeshift is installed.
You may need to run @code{timeshift --list} manually to verify snapshots.
@end table

@node Index
